Follow-up from the reporter: I've located this in Chromium's source, which answers the "is this Chromium's backend?" question in the body — **yes, it is**, and the code is already gone from Chromium `main`.

## Root cause

`chrome/browser/ui/views/status_icons/status_icon_linux_dbus.cc`, class **`StatusIconLinuxDbus::Multiplexer`**, as it exists on `refs/branch-heads/7871` (Chromium 150, which Electron 43.4.0 ships).

Chromium 150 exports **one shared** object at `/StatusNotifierItem` for every status icon in the process and demultiplexes incoming calls by the **destination field**:

```cpp
void Register(const std::string& service_name, StatusIconLinuxDbus* icon, dbus::Bus* bus) {
  icons_[service_name] = icon;                  // keyed by the WELL-KNOWN name
}

StatusIconLinuxDbus* GetIcon(const std::string& service_name) {
  auto it = icons_.find(service_name);
  return it != icons_.end() ? it->second : nullptr;
}

static void OnGetAllProperties(dbus::MethodCall* method_call,
                               dbus::ExportedObject::ResponseSender response_sender) {
  std::string destination = method_call->GetDestination();
  auto* icon = Get()->GetIcon(destination);
  if (!icon) {
    std::move(response_sender).Run(nullptr);    // ← becomes "error occurred in GetAll"
    return;
  }
  ...
}
```

`service_name_` is `org.freedesktop.StatusNotifierItem-<pid>-<id>`. A call addressed to the connection's unique name resolves to `GetIcon(":1.NNN")` → `nullptr` → `Run(nullptr)`, which libdbus surfaces as `Error.Failed: error occurred in GetAll` — the exact string in the report.

This also explains the asymmetry noted in the body: **`DbusMenu` is a separate per-icon exported object** that never goes through the `Multiplexer`, which is why the menu answers both addresses while `/StatusNotifierItem`'s `Properties` answers only one.

## A second, quieter bug in the same class

`OnActivate`, `OnContextMenu`, `OnScroll` and `OnSecondaryActivate` handle the same `nullptr` case **differently** — they return **success** and silently do nothing:

```cpp
auto* icon = Get()->GetIcon(destination);
if (icon) { ... }                                              // no else branch
std::move(response_sender).Run(dbus::Response::FromMethodCall(method_call));
```

So a host that resolves to the unique name gets a **dead click with an OK reply** and nothing to log. I initially misread this as working, because `Activate` via the unique name does return `()`. This is a separate contribution to "clicking the tray icon does nothing", independent of the unreadable `Menu` property, and it is harder to diagnose precisely because nothing fails.

## Handler-level measurements

Extending the table in the body — same process, same moment, only the destination differs:

| Call | via well-known | via unique `:1.NNN` |
|---|---|---|
| `Peer.Ping` | OK | OK |
| `Introspectable.Introspect` on `/StatusNotifierItem` | OK | OK |
| `Properties.GetAll` (`org.kde.StatusNotifierItem`) | OK | **FAIL** |
| `Properties.Get(Status)` | OK | **FAIL** |
| `org.kde.StatusNotifierItem.Activate` | OK | **OK, but silently no-ops** |
| `Properties.GetAll` on the menu object | OK | OK |

It is not the connection, not the object export, and not `Properties` generally — only the `Properties` implementation bound to the multiplexed `/StatusNotifierItem` object.

## Suggested fix

`main` has **no `Multiplexer` and no `GetDestination`** in that file — it is back to per-icon exported objects with methods exported directly on `item_`. So this looks like a backport/milestone question rather than a design to argue for:

1. **Preferred:** take `main`'s per-icon exported objects, which removes the destination assumption entirely.
2. **Minimal backport-safe fix:** resolve the destination to its owning connection before lookup, or fall back to the sole registered icon when `icons_.size() == 1` (the common case).
3. **Worth fixing regardless:** make the four action handlers return an error when `icon == nullptr` instead of a silent success.

Happy to refile on the Chromium tracker if that's the right home — just say the word.
